## Context (D-B96, `doc/plan/c016-worklog/decisions.ko.md`)
Reciprocal HANDOVER collapses to one direction by RID comparison (`core/src/runtime/sockets/router/router_admission.cpp:302-335`);
the losing direction's lanes stay as standby (`:364-379`, `:398-416`, `_standby_pipes`) and a REQUEST admitted on the losing
direction is pinned to that pair (`socket_request_reply_submit_api.cpp:121-180` pair/generation fence) and today ends only at its
own timeout with `ZLINK_REQUEST_TIMED_OUT`. The public contract test `core/tests/integration/test_router_reciprocal_handover_lanes.cpp:395-434`
pins that timeout. Same-direction HANDOVER (a reconnecting pipe takes over the existing one) strands requests the same way.

## What to do
1. At the point where a pair is superseded (reciprocal collapse and same-direction takeover), complete every pending REQUEST
   pinned to the superseded pair exactly once with `ZLINK_REQUEST_NOT_CONNECTED` + `EHOSTUNREACH` (existing result 109),
   through the normal completion record path (poller drains it like any request terminal). Keep the pair/generation reply
   fence, the standby pipe behaviour, and reply pinning unchanged. One owner: the router admission/handover code decides
   supersession; the request-reply state machine completes — do not add a second bookkeeping structure if the pending pool
   already indexes by pair.
2. Contract tests: update `test_router_reciprocal_handover_lanes.cpp` (losing request → `REQUEST_NOT_CONNECTED`, completion
   observed well before its 2 s timeout, e.g. < 200 ms; the resend on the winning direction succeeds) and add the same-direction
   takeover case (tcp + inproc). Keep the D-B94 handover latency test green.
3. Gate: `ctest --test-dir core/build-dev -j2` in the worktree (full), the CONTRIBUTING §5 checks, and — because this touches the
   request hot path — the hotpath policy check if `scripts/` provides one (report numbers; do not tune).
4. Deliverable: `doc/plan/c016-worklog/core-handover-stranded-request-summary.md` in the MAIN tree (/home/hep7/project/zlink):
